WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:07.219
In this video, we'll look at how we can package our game into an executable for distribution.

2
00:00:07.219 --> 00:00:13.019
To do this, we're going to open our Project Settings and look for the Packaging section.

3
00:00:13.019 --> 00:00:23.199
Come here to Edit, Project Settings, and come down to Packaging.

4
00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:33.700
To do this, we'll open our Project Settings in order to set the maps we want to include and any additional settings for our target platforms.

5
00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:43.259
Inside of Unreal, we can open the Project Settings from the Edit menu and clicking Project Settings.

6
00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:49.719
For this, we are only going to be making sure that the default game map is the one that we have been working on.

7
00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:55.159
However, if you want to set additional levels to be included in a build like you would in Unity,

8
00:00:55.159 --> 00:01:00.859
that can be found in the Advanced Options of the Packaging section in the Project Settings.

9
00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:07.959
You can also set in the Packaging section whether you want your build to be a development, testing, or shipping build.

10
00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:16.640
And if you want to set specific platform settings, you can find those further down the Project Settings list.

11
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:25.680
So here in our Project Settings, we want to go to Maps and Modes and make sure our session level is set as our default game map.

12
00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:31.420
If you have any other maps that you want to include in a larger project,

13
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:40.030
you can specify them here in the Advanced Settings of Packaging, all the way down here, a list of maps to include.

14
00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:52.790
We can also see in Packaging, we'll close the Advanced, what kind of build configuration we are going for

15
00:01:52.790 --> 00:02:04.299
and our platform settings for things like Windows or Android, Mac, etc., can be found here in the Platform Settings.

16
00:02:04.299 --> 00:02:12.039
Building our project can be done from the Platforms dropdown, where you then select the platform that you want to build for and hit Package.

17
00:02:12.039 --> 00:02:17.259
You can also change the build type in this dropdown as well, but I'll mention that anecdotally,

18
00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:29.520
I have found that the setting you specify in the Packaging section of the Project Settings can override whatever you pick in this dropdown.

19
00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:36.319
So let's go ahead, we'll go Platforms, Windows, Package Project.

20
00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:47.639
I'm going to go to this folder right here, Builds, Select, and we'll let it do its thing.

21
00:02:47.639 --> 00:02:55.530
The first time packaging your project can take a long time, but the following build times after that should be much faster.

22
00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:03.099
And we're done. All right, so let's go ahead and test it out.

23
00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:10.229
Looks like we can move, and our interactions are working. Fantastic.

24
00:03:10.229 --> 00:03:13.289
So we didn't give ourselves a way to quit.

25
00:03:13.289 --> 00:03:18.129
Quick and easy way to do that, though, is going to be the old Alt-F4.

26
00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:27.430
In the next video, we'll wrap up by taking an overview of everything we've covered today, as well as giving you some new topics to jump into next.
